---
// /roadmap — development status across the two public repositories.
//
// Rewritten 2026-09-19 after auditing every item against the repositories
// themselves rather than against the previous version of this page. What the
// audit found is why the page now looks the way it does:
//
//   * Four items were understated. Twelve product groups were described as
//     three; electronics and four "horizon" groups had already shipped.
//   * Two were overstated. An operator dashboard was listed as in build and
//     does not exist in any form, and the CLI — which is the actual control
//     plane — was not mentioned at all.
//   * Two were miscategorised in a way that mattered. The seal and registry
//     work is finished and legally inert, which is not the same fact as
//     "in progress", and a column reading "in build" said the wrong thing.
//   * Two prose claims were false. This page said it was reviewed fortnightly,
//     and linked to a public project board that does not exist.
//
// The rule the page now holds itself to: every item names something a reader
// can check without asking us, and no item is written in the grammar of a
// stronger claim than it can support.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import OurRoadmap from "../components/Roadmap.astro";
import data from "../data/roadmap.json";

// Counted from the vendored manifests rather than written down, so the figure
// cannot drift from the library the way "battery, textile and steel" did.
const manifests = import.meta.glob("../data/product-groups/*.json", { eager: true });
const groups = Object.values(manifests).map((m: any) => m.default ?? m);
const groupCount = groups.length;
const pluginCount = new Set(groups.filter((g) => g.plugin).map((g) => g.plugin)).size;

const { versions } = data as unknown as {
  versions: Record<string, { repo: string; version: string; note: string }>;
};

const title = "Roadmap — what is built, blocked and not started | Odal Node";
const description =
  "Development status for the Odal Node EU Digital Product Passport software: what ships today in dpp-core and dpp-engine, what is built but legally inert, what is in build, and the gaps we have not closed.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Section eyebrow="Public roadmap" title="What is built, what is blocked, and what is not started." wide>
    <p>
      Two public repositories: <a href="https://github.com/odal-node/dpp-core">dpp-core</a>,
      the regulatory library, and <a href="https://github.com/odal-node/dpp-engine">dpp-engine</a>,
      the software that runs a node. This page is the status of both, audited
      against the repositories rather than against the last version of this page.
    </p>

    <div class="not-prose my-8 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
      {Object.values(versions).map((v) => (
        <div class="card p-5">
          <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-neutral-500">{v.repo}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900">v{v.version}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-neutral-600">{v.note}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
      <div class="card p-5">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-neutral-500">product groups</p>
        <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900">{groupCount}</p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-neutral-600">
          {pluginCount} sandboxed rule plugins ·{" "}
          <a href="/product-groups" class="text-primary-700">see them all</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      <strong>Nobody is running this in production.</strong> No customers, no
      pilots, no live deployments, and nothing on this page should be read as
      evidence of any. The battery passport obligation begins on 18 February
      2027, and we would rather be correct when it arrives than early and wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are no dates below. Timing on regulatory infrastructure depends on
      delegated acts, standards-body votes and registry availability — none of
      which we control — so the category is the commitment and a quarter would
      be a guess dressed as one.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Status" title="Four registers, never mixed." tone="muted" wide>
    <div class="not-prose">
      <OurRoadmap />
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="How this is maintained" title="What moves a feature between registers.">
    <p>
      An item is <strong>shipped</strong> when it is in a tagged release of the
      relevant repository and a test exercises it through the running system.
      Merged is not shipped, and neither is demonstrated — "shipped" here means
      there is something that fails if it stops working.
    </p>
    <p>
      An item is <strong>in build</strong> only with a named condition attached,
      so that a reader can tell what would have to be true for it to move. An
      item on the <strong>horizon</strong> is an option under consideration, and
      is written in the grammar of an option: it carries no date, and no promise.
    </p>
    <p>
      The fourth register is the one most roadmaps do not have.{" "}
      <strong>Built and legally inert</strong> covers work that is finished,
      tested and running, and that cannot be used for the thing it exists to do.
      The sealing subsystem is the example: it works end to end and every seal it
      produces reports itself as self-issued and of no legal effect, because
      under eIDAS only a legal person may create an electronic seal. Filing that
      under "in build" would turn a legal fact into an engineering timeline.
    </p>
    <p>
      Items move backwards. Discovering while building something that it needs
      more foundational work is an ordinary outcome of building it, and that work
      returns to the horizon with a note about what was learned. A reversal
      recorded is better than an item quietly drifting for six months.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Honesty" title="What this page is not." tone="tinted">
    <p>
      It is not a schedule, and it is not exhaustive. The open issues in both
      repositories are public and carry the detail this page compresses —
      including the defects. If something you need is absent here, the useful
      assumption is that it does not exist rather than that it was forgotten,
      and the issue trackers are the place to say so.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/odal-node/dpp-core/issues">dpp-core issues</a>{" "}
      ·{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/odal-node/dpp-engine/issues">dpp-engine issues</a>
    </p>
  </Section>
</Base>
